Subject: Take acyclic function bytecodes off the collector's object list

What this does
  Keeps function bytecodes that hold no reference to a GC object that could take
  part in a cycle on a second list the collector does not walk, marked by a bit in
  the allocator header.

Why we need it
  The collector visits each code object twice per collection to account for one
  edge, and a code object with an empty constant pool, no object-literal table and
  no inline-cache table cannot take part in a cycle.

What it adds
  The predicate is evaluated when the object is created and re-evaluated where one
  of its terms changes, never per collection, and the default is traced. The realm
  reference stays counted, so a context cannot be freed while code names it; the
  untraced population is spliced back before the final collection, and admission
  stops once a second context exists on the runtime. A traced pass does not relink
  an untraced object that reached zero, an untraced object released during cycle
  removal is queued so it is still freed, and the header bit is zeroed wherever a
  block is born and carried when a block is relocated. No opcode is added or
  retired and BC_VERSION is unchanged.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

